## Add

There are various methods how to get {{ project.name }} into your project.
One common denominator is that you will
`#include <{{ project.repo }}/{{ project.repo }}.h>` in your sources and link
with `-l{{ project.repo }}`.


### Add as a dependency with mulle-sde

Use [mulle-sde](//github.com/mulle-sde) to add {{ project.repo }} to your project:

``` sh
mulle-sde add {{ project.domain }}:{{ project.user }}/{{ project.repo }}
```

This library does not include [mulle-atinit](//github.com/mulle-core/mulle-atinit)
and [mulle-atexit](//github.com/mulle-core/mulle-atexit) and
[mulle-testallocator](//github.com/mulle-core/mulle-testallocator). If you
add these libraries, it is important that mulle-core is added before them.


{% if not config.skipClibAdd %}

To only add the sources of {{ project.repo }} with dependency
sources use [clib](https://github.com/clibs/clib):


### Add sources to your project with clib

``` sh
clib install --out src/{{ project.user }} {{ project.user }}/{{ project.repo }}
```

Add `-isystem src/{{ project.user }}` to your `CFLAGS` and compile all the
sources that were downloaded with your project. (In **cmake** add
`include_directories( BEFORE SYSTEM src/{{ project.user }})` to your `CMakeLists.txt`
file).

{% endif %}


{%
   identifier = [[project.repo uppercaseString] stringByReplacingOccurrencesOfString:@"-" withString:@"__"];
   tag        = [identifier stringByAppendingString:@"_TAG"];
   tagref     = [@"${" stringByAppendingString:tag];
   tagref     = [tagref stringByAppendingString:@"}"];
%}
### Add as subproject with cmake

[mulle-core](//github.com/{{ project.user }}/mulle-core) is **not** bundled
with {{ project.repo }}. Add both with
[FetchContent](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/module/FetchContent.html):

``` cmake
include( FetchContent)

set( MULLE__CORE_TAG "master" CACHE STRING "branch or tag to fetch mulle-core from")

FetchContent_Declare( MULLE__CORE
   GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/{{ project.user }}/mulle-core.git
   GIT_TAG        "${MULLE__CORE_TAG}")
FetchContent_MakeAvailable( MULLE__CORE)

set( {{ identifier }}_TAG "master" CACHE STRING "branch or tag to fetch {{ project.repo }} from")

FetchContent_Declare( {{ identifier }}
   GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/{{ project.user }}/{{ project.repo }}.git
   GIT_TAG        "{{ tagref }}")
FetchContent_MakeAvailable( {{ identifier }})

target_link_libraries( ${PROJECT_NAME} PRIVATE {{ project.repo }})
```

`FetchContent_MakeAvailable( MULLE__CORE)` must come **before**
`FetchContent_MakeAvailable( {{ identifier }})`, because {{ project.repo }} depends on
the `mulle-core` target and its headers.

{{ project.repo }} is a **force-linkable** initialization library: it registers
`atinit`/`atexit` callbacks that must end up in your binary even if no symbol of
the library is referenced directly. The target created by
`FetchContent_MakeAvailable` carries the necessary `INTERFACE` force-link options,
so the `target_link_libraries( ${PROJECT_NAME} PRIVATE {{ project.repo }})` above
is sufficient. When linking {{ project.repo }} as an installed static library
instead, force-link it explicitly:

``` sh
# Linux (GCC/Clang)
-Wl,--whole-archive -l{{ project.repo }} -Wl,--no-whole-archive

# macOS
-force_load lib{{ project.repo }}.a

# Windows (MSVC)
/WHOLEARCHIVE:{{ project.repo }}.lib
```

